WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.230
Seventeen percent of truckers have never
been paid a single dollar of detention.

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.660
Not because they didn't wait.

3
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.750
Because they couldn't prove it.

4
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.200
In the next six minutes, I'll show you
exactly how to prove it — every time.

5
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.070
Detention isn't a nuisance.

6
00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.030
It is the single biggest uncompensated
cost in trucking.

7
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:23.104
Fifteen point one billion dollars a year,
according to the American Transportation

8
00:00:23.104 --> 00:00:24.280
Research Institute.

9
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:28.897
Eleven and a half billion in lost
productivity, three point six billion in

10
00:00:28.897 --> 00:00:29.770
added expense.

11
00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:34.780
Broken down per driver, that's eleven to
nineteen thousand dollars a year, gone.

12
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.810
Thirty-nine percent of deliveries hit
detention.

13
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.986
And of the detention that actually gets
billed, DAT reports fewer than half is

14
00:00:42.986 --> 00:00:43.650
ever paid.

15
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.360
And it isn't only money.

16
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.834
The Department of Transportation's
Inspector General found that every fifteen

17
00:00:49.834 --> 00:00:54.250
minutes of additional dwell time raises
crash risk by six point two percent.

18
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.830
ATRI found drivers speed fourteen point
six percent more after being detained.

19
00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.970
The time you lose at the dock, you try to
make up on the road.

20
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:06.710
If this is happening to you, you are not
the exception.

21
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.220
Seventy-two percent of owner-operators
wait over two hours every single week.

22
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.430
Fifty-nine percent of all drivers were
detained in the last two weeks.

23
00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:19.680
Eighty percent of them then struggled to
stay legal on hours of service.

24
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.270
This is the industry's normal.

25
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.950
It should not be yours.

26
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.650
Now the part nobody says out loud.

27
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.470
Detention bills at about sixty-three
dollars an hour on average.

28
00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:34.327
The marginal cost of operating a truck is
sixty-six dollars and sixty-five cents an

29
00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:34.620
hour.

30
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.501
Which means even when detention gets paid
in full, you're still three dollars and

31
00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:41.670
sixty-five cents an hour underwater.

32
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.930
Detention is not profit.

33
00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.450
It's damage control.

34
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:48.050
Which is exactly why you have to collect
all of it.

35
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.210
So here is the system.

36
00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:49.800
Five rules.

37
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:55.250
Follow all five, and detention stops being
a favour the broker does for you.

38
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.680
It becomes a bill they owe.

39
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:57.590
Rule one.

40
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.280
Get it in writing, before you book.

41
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.390
Verbal doesn't exist.

42
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.740
Text barely does.

43
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.400
It goes on the rate confirmation.

44
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.387
Write it yourself if you have to —
detention accrues two hours after

45
00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:14.136
appointment, seventy-five dollars an hour
— and have the broker sign that version

46
00:02:14.136 --> 00:02:14.840
back to you.

47
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.910
As one veteran owner-operator put it: if
there is no agreement at all, they can

48
00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.660
deny your detention, and there is
practically nothing you can do about it.

49
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.389
And watch for the trap — if the rate
confirmation says accessorial pay is

50
00:02:29.389 --> 00:02:33.730
included in the linehaul rate, you just
signed your detention away.

51
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:34.650
Rule two.

52
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.310
The clock starts at your on-time arrival.

53
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.290
Free time is standard at two hours, but it
assumes you showed up on time.

54
00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.720
Arrive late, and you've handed them the
defence for free.

55
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.620
Know the exact appointment time, and be
early enough to prove it.

56
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.400
Some facilities run one hour, some run
four.

57
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.550
It's on the rate confirmation — read it
before you roll.

58
00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.130
Rule three.

59
00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.680
Notify before free time ends, not after.

60
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.780
A message sent at hour one forty-five is
evidence.

61
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.520
A message sent at hour five is a
complaint.

62
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.420
Brokers need a contemporaneous record —
something created while it was actually

63
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.040
happening.

64
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.780
Send it in writing, and keep the
timestamp.

65
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.610
This single habit is the difference
between a paid claim and a denied one.

66
00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:19.140
Rule four.

67
00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.490
Five pieces of evidence, every time.

68
00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.510
GPS-stamped arrive and depart.

69
00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.520
A gate photo with a visible timestamp.

70
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.630
The bill of lading with in and out times,
initialled by the facility.

71
00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.490
The gate ticket or receipt.

72
00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:35.260
And a screenshot of the notice you sent
before free time ended.

73
00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:36.250
Rule five.

74
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:38.690
Invoice it immediately, with the load.

75
00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:42.940
Late invoicing is the number one reason
valid detention never gets paid.

76
00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.778
Bill it in the same invoice as the
linehaul — not as a separate favour weeks

77
00:03:47.778 --> 00:03:48.160
later.

78
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.103
Because once the load is off your truck,
the urgency about making you happy is

79
00:03:53.103 --> 00:03:53.420
over.

80
00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:58.536
Give the broker about two days to return
an updated rate confirmation, then invoice

81
00:03:58.536 --> 00:03:59.460
in full anyway.

82
00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.460
Now the part a corporate channel won't
tell you.

83
00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.570
Roughly half of brokers will pay it, if
your paperwork is clean.

84
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:11.120
About a quarter will negotiate — expect to
meet in the middle.

85
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.130
And about a quarter will never pay, no
matter what you send.

86
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.950
That last quarter is not a paperwork
problem.

87
00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.990
It's a customer problem.

88
00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.770
Quote them higher, or stop hauling for
them.

89
00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.310
Both are legitimate business decisions.

90
00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:30.415
The seventy-five percent you can actually
win is worth eight to fourteen thousand

91
00:04:30.415 --> 00:04:31.360
dollars a year.

92
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.630
That is the whole point.

93
00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.580
We built those five rules into the load
itself.

94
00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:40.641
Terms are on the rate confirmation before
you book — sixty dollars an hour after two

95
00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:42.690
hours free, printed on every load.

96
00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.840
Your arrival is GPS-stamped at the gate:
measured, not argued.

97
00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.458
You get an alert thirty minutes before
free time ends, and one tap sends the

98
00:04:51.458 --> 00:04:53.160
broker a timestamped notice.

99
00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.454
Then the claim builds itself with the
evidence attached, and lands on your

100
00:04:57.454 --> 00:04:58.730
invoice automatically.

101
00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.490
One hundred percent of every accessorial
passes through to you.

102
00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:05.350
We take five percent of the linehaul.

103
00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:06.490
Nothing else.

104
00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:06.970
LoadBoot.

105
00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:08.730
Detention pay that pays.

106
00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.570
Free verified accounts, flat five percent
dispatch, zero monthly.

107
00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.210
Everything at loadboot dot com.

